From 6735c9f4316a518518b0bf23a66217e49f64eef7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: barracuda156 <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 22:17:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] simdjson: gate the PPC64 VMX kernel behind an explicit opt-in

Companion to the simdutf VSX gate, with a different and narrower
rationale.  Unlike simdutf's kernel, simdjson's PPC64 kernel needs
only VMX, which every 64-bit PowerPC that could run this has (970 and
later), and the kernel was written with big-endian in mind: the SIMD
stages carry endian conditionals throughout, and number parsing
auto-disables the SWAR fast path on big-endian in every
numberparsing_defs (which also makes the Darwin-less bswap ladder in
ppc64/numberparsing_defs.h unreachable dead code rather than a bug).

What remains is that upstream CI only ever exercises the kernel on
little-endian POWER, and SIMDJSON_CAN_ALWAYS_RUN_PPC64 means whatever
is compiled in gets selected with no runtime check.  Since __ALTIVEC__
appears as soon as a cpu-tuning variant passes -mcpu=970 or
-mcpu=native, require an explicit SIMDJSON_ENABLE_PPC64_VMX until a
big-endian build of the kernel has been validated once; without it
such builds keep the portable fallback, which is big-endian-correct by
construction (SWAR off, per-digit parsing).

Note the gate changes nothing on builds from this port today: Darwin
gcc defines __ppc64__ but not __PPC64__, so the branch never fires on
darwin regardless.  This is documentation-grade armor for toolchains
and targets that do define __PPC64__ (e.g. linux-BE), and the flip is
one define.  The 32-bit build never reaches any of this.
---
 deps/simdjson/simdjson.cpp | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/deps/simdjson/simdjson.cpp b/deps/simdjson/simdjson.cpp
index 8f33059..43c3e88 100644
--- a/deps/simdjson/simdjson.cpp
+++ b/deps/simdjson/simdjson.cpp
@@ -240,7 +240,13 @@ using std::size_t;
 #endif
 #elif defined(__PPC64__) || defined(_M_PPC64)
 #define SIMDJSON_IS_PPC64 1
-#if defined(__ALTIVEC__)
+// VMX itself is available on every 64-bit PowerPC that could run this
+// (970 and later), and the kernel carries big-endian conditionals - but
+// upstream CI only ever exercises it on little-endian POWER, and there
+// is no runtime gate (CAN_ALWAYS_RUN): whatever is compiled in gets
+// selected.  Keep the CI-unexercised endianness opt-in until a
+// big-endian build has been validated once.
+#if defined(__ALTIVEC__) && defined(SIMDJSON_ENABLE_PPC64_VMX)
 #define SIMDJSON_IS_PPC64_VMX 1
 #endif // defined(__ALTIVEC__)
 #else
-- 
2.43.0

